# 0111 WIP: stop resetting the EP2 endpoint when the host is not reading.
#
# 0110 kept the QMU ring populated with four buffers and TX-done driven
# refills, but it still had the 0.4 s EP_RST watchdog inherited from 0100.
# Before the host opened EP2, that watchdog reset the endpoint 27 times: the
# queue filled, no TX done arrived because nobody was reading, the watchdog
# flushed the FIFO, and the driver refilled.  When the host finally read, it
# saw a reordered, gap-filled stream (77, 82, 79, 80, 81) and got EPIPE after
# five packets; the QMU was left inactive.
#
# The bare-metal P9 path never reset the endpoint on a timeout: it only wrote
# QMU_Q_RESUME every two seconds and waited.  This version does the same for
# the timeout, keeping EP_RST for explicit QMU errors and CLEAR_FEATURE.
# The timeout is also armed only after the first TX done: before the host has
# issued an IN token, no TX done is expected on a bulk IN endpoint, so the
# queue stays silent instead of being poked or reset while it waits.
# The queue model, 63-byte frames, TX_SLOT(2), EP0 and the vendor status /
# diagnostic requests are unchanged.
#
# Acceptance: while the host is not reading, the recovery counter must stay
# flat; once it reads, at least 100 consecutive packets with no gaps and no
# duplicates.

MACOS_VERSION=13.5
KERNEL_CONFIG=DEVELOPMENT
MACHINE_CONFIG=SWEET

BASE_REPO=https://github.com/MakrSas/ios-topaz.git
BASE_SHA=5095ac19dbdcf37b64b81fe443f4027ad51b84bf
PATCHSET_SHA256=472744f19c2357ca5149cb33ea1ee9a37266af3b4f83065a54cfab4d17eb353d
BASELINE_CHANGES_SHA256=1edb66332bb4daa8b1abcd6c4de77a0e88ed9c7e9bb4a2a7045a2696e53ab338

DXB_REPO=https://github.com/blacktop/darwin-xnu-build
DXB_SHA=663906528d1cd5ea8fde7de6f16de73db9511549

KERNEL_EXPECT_EXEC_DATA=
CI_RUN_ID=
CI_KERNEL_SHA256=
CI_KERNEL_EXEC_DATA_SHA256=
CI_IMAGE_SHA256=

BOOT_CMDLINE=config/0111-boot.cmdline
